WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.680
Obviously, the pandemic,

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.530
but there's been so much other heaviness

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:06.840
going on in the last month.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.330
I know that you'd been a activist

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:13.940
and a very active activist
for many, many years,

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:16.406
and you take this very seriously,

00:00:16.406 --> 00:00:18.323
and I'm just curious,

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:23.160
how have the conversations been

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:28.110
in the wake of George Floyd's murder?

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.390
Were you and your family
having different conversations

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:32.660
than you've had before,

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:35.996
or was it more intense,

00:00:35.996 --> 00:00:37.754
or did you feel like, no,

00:00:37.754 --> 00:00:39.820
this didn't change anything for us,

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.500
we've known this for a long time.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.870
Well, we've definitely had
these conversations before,

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:50.870
but I think everyone has felt
an intensity about this one

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.123
and it has been ratcheted up,

00:00:55.991 --> 00:00:58.380
mainly because of the callousness

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.590
and the manner in which
the murder happened,

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:05.220
and how we were all locked down

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.160
and just watching it on a loop.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.208
I mean, I never saw the
whole tape, actually.

00:01:09.208 --> 00:01:11.970
I just saw the beginning and the end.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.023
I couldn't really watch it.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:19.860
But, yes, these conversations
have been had prior to this

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.743
with my family, with the community,

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:26.090
but with my kids, it was
definitely ratcheted up a lot more,

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.870
because, for them, my youngest anyway,

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:32.040
is really just getting into the workforce,

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.680
just beginning her life,

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.570
and really starting her
life as an adult out there,

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:40.113
and having all of these things
happen at the same time.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.110
George Floyd was after Ahmaud Arbery,

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:46.000
was after Breonna Taylor,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.440
was after even Amy Cooper in the park.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.740
So, I think the confluence of
all of those things together,

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.676
with the pandemic on top of it,

00:01:54.676 --> 00:01:59.310
really made this boiling point happen,

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:02.490
and then we saw the lid come off,

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:07.270
and, hopefully, we will
continue to find ways

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.180
to keep the conversation alive.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:12.080
I know that there's still
protests, I mean, Portland,

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.630
it's still happening
all around the country,

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:15.440
all around the world really.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.890
So it's not like people
have completely forgotten.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:21.650
But human beings want to exhale.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.610
They want to let that stress off

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.700
and go back to what
they believe is normal.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.880
But I just think we can't do that now

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.740
because I think a return to what is normal

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:32.160
is actually a step back.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.180
It won't be just a return,
it'll be a complete reset

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.246
to something that I don't
think we can afford to do

00:02:39.246 --> 00:02:43.420
as a community, so, hopefully,
this moment is instructive

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:46.040
and, hopefully, we'll find
many, many different ways

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:47.300
to continue this conversation

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.530
and find out how we can really approach

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.733
this systematic issue
from all different angles.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.190
There's a question I wanted to ask you

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.550
that I've been pondering a little bit,

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:05.963
which is the role of
celebrities in all of this.

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:12.180
I think it can, maybe, sometimes
get a little complicated

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.540
with celebrities.
100%.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:16.940
And I think you feel the same way,

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.823
but I thought maybe we could
have a discussion about that.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:25.344
My feeling being there's a very fine

00:03:25.344 --> 00:03:30.344
between someone in the public
eye giving of themselves

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.680
and trying to move this piece forward,

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.853
and trying to help us all grow,

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:41.140
and saying, "hey, look
at me, check me out."

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.940
Yeah.
It can flip so quickly

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:47.940
and I just wanted to talk to you about it,

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:51.710
'cause I'm sure you have
a strong opinion about it.

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:53.080
Well, I agree with what you're saying.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:55.340
I mean, it can be a double-edged sword.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:00.340
We have this platform and
we're both sensitive people,

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.290
and we are a part of the world,

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:08.330
and we're understand that
though we have this ability

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:11.845
to, in some ways, insulate
ourselves from things.

00:04:11.845 --> 00:04:14.090
At the same time, we are not insulated

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:17.070
and these issues affect everyone,

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.980
and, so, yes, we want
to take our platforms

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.780
and find ways to be productive

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.479
and find ways to actually
say move this thing forward.

00:04:24.479 --> 00:04:28.080
But I think a lot of times
what it requires is for us

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.710
to kind of step out of the way

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:35.340
and use the power that
we have as producers,

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.450
as people behind the cameras,

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:39.071
as well as in front of the camera

00:04:39.071 --> 00:04:42.410
to push it on that end,

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:45.650
and make sure that the
non-centered voices get centered,

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:48.550
and make sure that the
people who are not seen

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:49.720
get to the front,

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:52.060
and something that I saw people were doing

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:53.650
on Twitter which I
thought was really cool,

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:56.550
was a lot of people were just
giving their platform over

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.860
to other people, you
have a million followers,

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.070
you're like, okay, now
let's check out Debbie

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.010
who's doing this with whatever,

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:05.950
or Jim who's doing this over here,

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:09.560
and they're just gonna have
my platform for awhile.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.470
So, I think a lot of times
our best work is done

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:13.940
when we step out of the way.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:15.470
Yeah.
We get the attention

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:18.306
and then we shift the
light, shift the focus,

00:05:18.306 --> 00:05:21.210
and yeah, it's tricky
because you can look like

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:22.043
you're clout surfing,

00:05:22.043 --> 00:05:24.286
you can look like you're
trying to go, look at me,

00:05:24.286 --> 00:05:25.980
but I'm cool with saying look at me,

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:27.540
as long as once people
are looking at you go

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:29.080
now look at them.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:30.067
So-
Right, right, okay.

00:05:30.067 --> 00:05:31.040
That's a great way.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:33.100
You have to be practical
about that, right?

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.180
That's a great way of thinking of it.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:40.180
I like that, because I'm always...

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:44.410
and I also think it's,
be willing to try things,

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:47.803
have them work, not
work, but just move on.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.630
It's that fear that a lot of
people have of making a mistake

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:57.490
or looking silly that
gets discussed a lot now,

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:00.210
which is try and if you make a mistake,

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:02.990
or if some of your friends check you

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:04.660
and say "yeah, that wasn't quite it.

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:06.360
Nice try, but that wasn't quite it,"

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.490
don't overreact and get
defensive, try again.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:11.368
Yeah, fail better, I mean,

00:06:11.368 --> 00:06:14.800
I'm not sure whose the quote is,

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:16.850
I had a discussion with Eddie Escalad,

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:18.740
and if he wants to text
in, he can tell us,

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:22.600
but, yeah, I think it's
try, fail, fail better.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:27.600
Just keep at it, it's
too important to dip out,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:32.350
and especially because you
get beat up on Twitter,

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:36.870
or wherever, that's not
really the barometer

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.840
by which we should be judging
how we should be acting,

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.620
although I understand it can influence us.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.640
But we just have to
continue to ring the bell

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:47.670
and, like you said, and sometimes
it's not the perfect way,

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:49.580
but you just gotta keep ringing it.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:53.410
Yeah, I always think this has
been something that's been,

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:54.810
this issue has plagued our country

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:56.040
for a couple of hundred years,

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:00.240
it is the defining stain on our country

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:04.390
that we need to get
better, we need to resolve,

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:06.270
and so, if some embarrassment on Twitter

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.695
is the price that you pay, so what?

00:07:08.695 --> 00:07:10.142
Suck it up.
Yeah.

00:07:10.142 --> 00:07:12.633
Suck it up, suck it up.

